WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.334 --> 00:00:04.036
Agnes considers herself
one of the first ones here.

00:00:04.069 --> 00:00:06.706
Most people think
she's an insufferable snob,

00:00:06.739 --> 00:00:09.275
but I understand her
having played her now,

00:00:09.342 --> 00:00:10.510
<i> all these months.</i>

00:00:10.543 --> 00:00:12.344
♪ (DRAMATIC ORCHESTRAL MUSIC
PLAYING) ♪

00:00:12.378 --> 00:00:14.013
There's a big storyline

00:00:14.047 --> 00:00:15.781
in the arrival
of the Russell family

00:00:15.848 --> 00:00:17.583
<i> who are building a mansion</i>

00:00:17.616 --> 00:00:19.585
<i> across the street
 from my townhouse.</i>

00:00:19.619 --> 00:00:22.856
When my late husband
bought this, it was rural.

00:00:22.889 --> 00:00:24.958
Central Park was
just countryside.

00:00:25.024 --> 00:00:26.559
She doesn't know the Russells.

00:00:26.593 --> 00:00:28.928
She can't stand
what they're about.

00:00:29.228 --> 00:00:32.631
<i> It's all manipulation
 and ostentation,</i>

00:00:32.698 --> 00:00:34.934
and it's everything
that I abhor.

00:00:34.968 --> 00:00:36.269
Judging by your aunt's face,

00:00:36.302 --> 00:00:38.404
it'll be a while
before you see me here again.

00:00:38.438 --> 00:00:39.472
(BOTH CHUCKLING)

00:00:39.906 --> 00:00:41.941
CHRISTINE BARANSKI:
<i> I give Marian a hard time.</i>

00:00:42.208 --> 00:00:44.376
She's my brother's daughter

00:00:44.410 --> 00:00:46.445
and I know
what my brother was like

00:00:46.579 --> 00:00:48.647
<i> and it led to his ruin.</i>

00:00:48.714 --> 00:00:51.084
<i> You lived or died
 by your reputation.</i>

00:00:51.484 --> 00:00:54.720
I feel that Agnes
has her best interests in mind.
I really do.

00:00:54.754 --> 00:00:57.290
I know more about these things
than you give me credit for.

00:00:57.323 --> 00:01:00.326
BARANSKI:<i> My brother was
 supposed to manage the estate,</i>

00:01:00.392 --> 00:01:01.828
<i> but he squandered it.</i>

00:01:02.261 --> 00:01:04.897
I really had to step up
and be the big sister.

00:01:04.931 --> 00:01:05.898
Money isn't everything.

00:01:05.931 --> 00:01:07.167
You say that to me

00:01:07.233 --> 00:01:09.736
after the sacrifice I made
to save your skin.

00:01:09.769 --> 00:01:12.338
There is undercurrent
of resentments.

00:01:12.405 --> 00:01:13.606
I'm very grateful.

00:01:13.639 --> 00:01:14.674
So you should be.

00:01:15.275 --> 00:01:18.277
I can see rather quickly
that Peggy is extremely smart.

00:01:18.444 --> 00:01:19.445
If you're interested,

00:01:19.478 --> 00:01:21.947
I would like to hire you
as my secretary.

00:01:22.281 --> 00:01:23.515
That does interest me.

00:01:23.582 --> 00:01:27.419
I can see that Peggy's journey
is a lot harder

00:01:27.653 --> 00:01:29.489
than my niece's is going to be.

00:01:29.522 --> 00:01:32.925
Agnes has had a tough life.
She respects anybody

00:01:32.958 --> 00:01:36.262
<i> who still is going to
 make something of herself.</i>

00:01:36.296 --> 00:01:38.197
Life has taught me one thing,
Ms. Scott.

00:01:38.764 --> 00:01:40.499
If you don't want
to be disappointed,

00:01:40.533 --> 00:01:42.968
only help those
who help themselves.

00:01:43.002 --> 00:01:45.604
That's the woman you see
at the beginning of the series,

00:01:45.638 --> 00:01:50.142
is a woman who's just had to
really make it on her own.

00:01:50.175 --> 00:01:55.215
I love keying into her
extraordinary strength
and certitude.

00:01:57.984 --> 00:02:00.386
Ada is an unmarried lady,

00:02:00.453 --> 00:02:02.855
what was called at that time
a spinster, an old maid.

00:02:02.889 --> 00:02:05.358
♪ (DELICATE MUSIC PLAYS) ♪

00:02:06.359 --> 00:02:10.463
She is the sister of Agnes,
who is a wealthy widow,

00:02:10.496 --> 00:02:15.734
and she is the aunt of Marian,
who is, much to Ada's delight,

00:02:15.801 --> 00:02:18.571
coming to live with
her two aunts in New York City.

00:02:18.638 --> 00:02:21.573
This is Miss Marian Brook,
our late brother's daughter.

00:02:21.640 --> 00:02:24.176
For Ada, who doesn't have
any children of her own,

00:02:24.210 --> 00:02:27.312
here is a chance
to be a mother of sorts.

00:02:27.346 --> 00:02:29.648
All we both want
is your happiness.

00:02:30.549 --> 00:02:34.154
You may disagree with us,
but that is all we want.

00:02:37.323 --> 00:02:40.092
Across the street
are the Russells,

00:02:40.159 --> 00:02:43.262
who are the new money,
and their house is amazing,

00:02:43.329 --> 00:02:44.897
<i> their carriage is amazing,</i>

00:02:44.930 --> 00:02:46.765
<i> their footmen's outfits
 are amazing.</i>

00:02:46.832 --> 00:02:47.767
<i> It's a palace,</i>

00:02:48.567 --> 00:02:51.437
<i> and Mr. and Mrs. Russell are
 industrialists and new money</i>

00:02:51.503 --> 00:02:53.673
and it's a fight between

00:02:53.706 --> 00:02:56.074
the old New York
and new New York.

00:02:56.241 --> 00:02:58.111
My mother, your grandmother,

00:02:58.378 --> 00:03:00.579
was a Livingston
of Livingston Manor.

00:03:00.613 --> 00:03:03.916
They ruled justly
until the new people invaded.

00:03:04.216 --> 00:03:06.286
It's not quite
as simple as that.

00:03:06.352 --> 00:03:07.353
Yes, it is.

00:03:07.386 --> 00:03:10.556
Agnes disapproves
of them greatly.

00:03:10.589 --> 00:03:13.693
<i> This is just like the devil
has moved in across the street.</i>

00:03:14.894 --> 00:03:16.762
<i> Ada isn't so sure.</i>

00:03:16.796 --> 00:03:19.098
She knows she shouldn't be
interested in them,

00:03:19.131 --> 00:03:23.236
<i> but she can't help
 but creep over to the window</i>

00:03:23.703 --> 00:03:26.973
<i> and look out the blinds
as they move into their new home</i>

00:03:27.039 --> 00:03:28.975
with all their finery.

00:03:29.041 --> 00:03:31.043
AGNES VAN RHIJN:<i> First,
 Mrs. Chamberlain and now them.</i>

00:03:31.076 --> 00:03:33.145
Why don't we just go outside
and roll in the gutter?

00:03:33.212 --> 00:03:34.547
It will save time.

00:03:34.580 --> 00:03:37.817
Remember, Agnes,
charity is the order of the day.

00:03:37.884 --> 00:03:40.419
Agnes is really afraid
of change,

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:43.323
<i> and Ada is eager for it.</i>

00:03:43.389 --> 00:03:44.991
She's at this moment in her life

00:03:45.057 --> 00:03:48.561
when things have just been
the same for a really long time,

00:03:48.594 --> 00:03:49.962
and there's nothing happening.

00:03:49.996 --> 00:03:52.298
<i> And now, things are afoot,</i>

00:03:52.331 --> 00:03:55.167
<i>and afoot not just in the world
 and just in New York,</i>

00:03:55.234 --> 00:03:58.237
but within her own family
and on her own street.

00:03:59.405 --> 00:04:02.742
LOUISA JACOBSON:<i> Marian sees
the world as full of brightness.</i>

00:04:02.775 --> 00:04:08.747
And what is so cool about Marian
is her strength of character.

00:04:08.781 --> 00:04:11.017 line:0%
♪ (CLASSICAL MUSIC PLAYING) ♪

00:04:12.284 --> 00:04:14.920
<i> We meet Marian
 when she's lost everything.</i>

00:04:15.187 --> 00:04:17.990
She's lost her home,
she's lost her father,

00:04:18.457 --> 00:04:20.426
<i> she's lost most
 of her possessions.</i>

00:04:20.827 --> 00:04:23.629
You will have somewhere
in the region of 30 dollars.

00:04:23.663 --> 00:04:25.931
JACOBSON:
<i> And she comes to New York.</i>

00:04:25.964 --> 00:04:29.434
<i> She's very vulnerable
 and doesn't have
 any footing anymore,</i>

00:04:29.468 --> 00:04:30.803
<i> except for in her aunts,</i>

00:04:30.836 --> 00:04:33.106
<i> and she's heard a lot
 of bad things about Agnes.</i>

00:04:33.139 --> 00:04:34.473
You are my niece.

00:04:35.007 --> 00:04:37.476
And you belong to old New York.

00:04:37.843 --> 00:04:42.281
Agnes immediately tells her that
she has to come out of mourning.

00:04:42.314 --> 00:04:45.651
You will go tomorrow with Ada
to my dressmaker, and no black.

00:04:46.118 --> 00:04:47.486
But I'm in mourning.

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:49.288
People here won't know
when Henry died.

00:04:49.321 --> 00:04:52.492
That's the first instance where
I really butt heads with her.

00:04:52.525 --> 00:04:54.860
AGNES:
<i>She can be so like him at times.</i>

00:04:54.893 --> 00:04:57.229
<i> She speaks
 and I hear Henry's voice.</i>

00:04:57.663 --> 00:04:59.798
<i> Challenging everything,
 just as he did.</i>

00:04:59.999 --> 00:05:01.634
And look how that turned out.

00:05:02.168 --> 00:05:03.736
Well, I like her strong views.

00:05:04.670 --> 00:05:06.039
I like her energy.

00:05:06.972 --> 00:05:10.242
Ada is more understanding of me.

00:05:10.642 --> 00:05:12.812
We also have a bond
over my father.

00:05:12.845 --> 00:05:16.148
You told me Aunt Agnes
hated my father because
he sold our family house.

00:05:16.181 --> 00:05:17.316
Do you feel the same?

00:05:18.184 --> 00:05:20.886
Agnes has always carried
the weight of the world
on her shoulders.

00:05:20.919 --> 00:05:23.222
Henry used to tease me
and make me laugh.

00:05:23.255 --> 00:05:24.390
So, no...

00:05:25.357 --> 00:05:26.992
I don't feel the same way.

00:05:27.426 --> 00:05:29.595
Peggy and Marian's relationship
is really interesting.

00:05:29.661 --> 00:05:31.264
I hope one day to write a novel.

00:05:31.731 --> 00:05:32.865
I envy you.

00:05:33.766 --> 00:05:37.170
I'm not like you.
I don't have any burning talent
yearning to be free.

00:05:37.203 --> 00:05:39.772
I just want to be busy,
to be needed.

00:05:40.205 --> 00:05:42.441
Peggy and Marian's
relationship grows from

00:05:42.508 --> 00:05:44.577
<i> a transactional moment</i>

00:05:45.044 --> 00:05:46.945
into a more meaningful
relationship where

00:05:47.012 --> 00:05:50.916
<i> Marian looks up to Peggy
 and is inspired by Peggy.</i>

00:05:50.950 --> 00:05:53.452
It ends up growing
into even more than that.

00:05:53.518 --> 00:05:54.920
Why don't you look in?

00:05:55.087 --> 00:05:56.789
Aunt Agnes would have a fit.

00:05:56.855 --> 00:05:59.391
I could wait downstairs to make
sure you can get back in.

00:05:59.725 --> 00:06:01.527
My fellow conspirator.

00:06:04.030 --> 00:06:06.466
DENÉE BENTON:<i> Something
 I think is so interesting
 about Peggy's character</i>

00:06:06.532 --> 00:06:08.600
is she's the one woman
we kind of get to see

00:06:08.801 --> 00:06:10.202
in the professional class.

00:06:10.236 --> 00:06:12.438
♪ (CLASSICAL MUSIC PLAYING) ♪

00:06:13.439 --> 00:06:16.943
<i>There was a newer class that was
 developing during that time.</i>

00:06:16.976 --> 00:06:19.912
What it meant to not be
in the service industry

00:06:19.945 --> 00:06:22.582
or to be
an inherited wealth aristocrat,

00:06:22.615 --> 00:06:24.817
<i> but to be a lawyer, a writer,
 an architect.</i>

00:06:24.883 --> 00:06:25.985
We see these different things.

00:06:26.585 --> 00:06:29.221
I do know you've taken a chance
on me, Mrs. van Rhijn.

00:06:29.721 --> 00:06:30.923
And I appreciate it.

00:06:31.490 --> 00:06:33.426
Life has taught me one thing,
Miss Scott.

00:06:33.993 --> 00:06:35.728
If you don't want
to be disappointed,

00:06:35.762 --> 00:06:38.131
only help those
who help themselves.

00:06:39.165 --> 00:06:42.468
She runs into every kind
of obstacle that you would face

00:06:42.501 --> 00:06:46.172
as someone who has the audacity
to dream of being a writer

00:06:46.238 --> 00:06:48.107
as a Black woman in that time.

00:06:48.140 --> 00:06:50.410
What other changes must I make
to be published?

00:06:50.977 --> 00:06:54.613
Your name is fine, but your race
would have to remain concealed.

00:06:54.647 --> 00:06:57.583
It's the best arrangement
I can offer.

00:06:57.616 --> 00:06:59.352
There are at least two white men

00:06:59.418 --> 00:07:00.620
sitting in a bar
around the corner

00:07:01.087 --> 00:07:04.023
drinking away their sorrows
because I turned them down.

00:07:04.990 --> 00:07:06.826
They'd kill to be
in your position.

00:07:08.494 --> 00:07:10.529
But they'd never be
in my position.

00:07:10.996 --> 00:07:13.766
We get to see Peggy have
these interesting moments

00:07:13.799 --> 00:07:14.667
downstairs in the basement,

00:07:15.368 --> 00:07:17.303
when Peggy is with the
service staff of that household.

00:07:17.336 --> 00:07:19.839
<i> And you're able to see
 the assumption</i>

00:07:20.106 --> 00:07:21.707
<i> that is made by all of them</i>

00:07:21.773 --> 00:07:24.110
that Peggy must be poor,

00:07:24.143 --> 00:07:26.678
Peggy must not have access to
these certain kinds of things.

00:07:27.212 --> 00:07:29.114
Have you been working
with Mrs. van Rhijn?

00:07:29.147 --> 00:07:30.048
Yes.

00:07:30.716 --> 00:07:32.518
I suppose you think that
makes you a sort of secretary.

00:07:32.551 --> 00:07:35.153
She is a secretary
and not a "sort of" anything,

00:07:35.187 --> 00:07:38.023
which puts her above you
in the pecking order,
Miss Armstrong.

00:07:38.724 --> 00:07:40.693
If it were the 1880s,
this would've been my story.

00:07:40.726 --> 00:07:43.562
The proximity that I walk today
felt very similar

00:07:43.829 --> 00:07:45.130
<i> to the proximity
 that she was walking,</i>

00:07:45.164 --> 00:07:46.632
<i>and it fell off the page to me.</i>

00:07:46.665 --> 00:07:49.335
I know what she's... carrying.

00:07:49.368 --> 00:07:52.004
You're a New Yorker now.
We both are.

00:07:52.738 --> 00:07:55.073
And for a New Yorker,
anything is possible.

00:07:56.141 --> 00:07:58.010
I think one of the most
challenging aspects

00:07:58.043 --> 00:08:01.146
of playing Oscar
has been keeping this mustache

00:08:01.180 --> 00:08:03.082
exactly the same shape
for over a year.

00:08:03.148 --> 00:08:05.050
♪ (CLASSICAL MUSIC PLAYS) ♪

00:08:05.084 --> 00:08:07.753
Oscar has definitely learned
the value

00:08:07.820 --> 00:08:10.389
of a good verbal laceration
from his mother.

00:08:10.422 --> 00:08:12.224
Stop talking to yourself
and ring the bell.

00:08:12.257 --> 00:08:14.526
-I'm going up to change.
-I doubt it, Mama.

00:08:14.893 --> 00:08:17.563
I'd say you will come down again
without having changed at all.

00:08:17.596 --> 00:08:19.865
In a world where almost everyone
tip toes around Agnes,

00:08:19.899 --> 00:08:21.400
terrified of courting
her displeasure,

00:08:22.034 --> 00:08:23.703
<i> Oscar is basically
 the only character</i>

00:08:23.736 --> 00:08:24.770
<i> who is fearless.</i>

00:08:25.071 --> 00:08:26.338
I think, fundamentally,
they're very close.

00:08:26.371 --> 00:08:29.508
Which is why she endures this.

00:08:29.541 --> 00:08:32.244
Agnes views New York
elite society

00:08:32.277 --> 00:08:34.947
as a small group of people
with notable genealogy.

00:08:35.014 --> 00:08:36.548
<i> And that's the way
 she would like it to remain.</i>

00:08:37.349 --> 00:08:39.351
<i> Oscar, I think can see this
 new money's going to have
 a greater influence,</i>

00:08:39.618 --> 00:08:41.354
and he wants to be a part
of it.

00:08:41.387 --> 00:08:43.188
OSCAR VAN RHIJN:
I think we should know
the Russell family.

00:08:43.221 --> 00:08:45.725
I would go further,
and say I think it is mad
that we don't.

00:08:45.758 --> 00:08:49.595
Money is of primordial
importance to Oscar.

00:08:49.628 --> 00:08:52.565
He is very much on the hunt
for an heiress.

00:08:52.598 --> 00:08:54.367
<i> He is independently wealthy,
 himself.</i>

00:08:55.067 --> 00:08:57.570
<i> He will one day inherit
 the whole Brook's household.</i>

00:08:57.603 --> 00:09:00.039
But he sees wealth
of a whole new magnitude,

00:09:00.072 --> 00:09:02.975
<i>and he has an insatiable hunger
 for the lifestyle</i>

00:09:03.042 --> 00:09:04.042
<i> that that will support.</i>

00:09:04.076 --> 00:09:05.744
I must have more money.

00:09:05.778 --> 00:09:08.046
I love all the minutiae
of etiquette

00:09:08.080 --> 00:09:09.148
and the behavioral codes.

00:09:10.249 --> 00:09:13.251
I've tried to break
as many of those rules
as possible in every scene.

00:09:13.919 --> 00:09:15.988
<i> So I play a character
 who has a hearty irreverence
 for those rules,</i>

00:09:16.055 --> 00:09:17.623
<i> especially when he's
 in the Brook's household.</i>

00:09:17.656 --> 00:09:19.758
I think he's aware that this
kind of charm is a commodity

00:09:19.792 --> 00:09:21.393
in polite New York society.

00:09:21.426 --> 00:09:23.762
<i> Verbal playfulness
 has a kind of currency to it.</i>

00:09:23.796 --> 00:09:25.731
Handsome young men who talk
are always useful.

00:09:25.764 --> 00:09:27.066
That's what my mother says.

00:09:27.099 --> 00:09:28.834
And a girl should always listen
to her mom.

00:09:29.434 --> 00:09:31.069
BLAKE RITSON:
<i>Oscar kind of goes through life</i>

00:09:31.103 --> 00:09:33.339
<i> considering it a game
 where you can make up
 your own rules.</i>

00:09:33.405 --> 00:09:35.741
<i> This is a world where
 most characters are very bound</i>

00:09:35.775 --> 00:09:38.277
by social conduct
and the codes of behavior

00:09:38.310 --> 00:09:40.112
and it's quite fun
to play a part

00:09:40.145 --> 00:09:42.081
who is happy to break
those rules.

00:09:42.315 --> 00:09:44.483
He'd much rather be happy
than good.

00:09:45.684 --> 00:09:50.255
Tom Raikes is a young,
confident, hardworking attorney

00:09:50.288 --> 00:09:51.857
from Doylestown, Pennsylvania.

00:09:51.923 --> 00:09:54.126
♪ (UPLIFTING MUSIC PLAYS) ♪

00:09:54.760 --> 00:09:57.262
THOMAS COCQUEREL:
<i> In episode one, we meet him
 and Marian Brook,</i>

00:09:57.596 --> 00:10:00.466
and I think at the start
it's a professional relationship

00:10:00.499 --> 00:10:03.302
but he quickly becomes
quite charmed by her.

00:10:03.335 --> 00:10:05.103
<i> Being put in that position
 as her attorney</i>

00:10:05.137 --> 00:10:09.374
and trying to help her
financially get back
on her feet,

00:10:09.441 --> 00:10:12.344
<i> he's the one who pushes her
 to move to New York</i>

00:10:12.377 --> 00:10:15.347
to have a safety net
and to be with her aunts.

00:10:15.715 --> 00:10:17.450
I'm sorry you're going,
Ms. Brook.

00:10:17.483 --> 00:10:19.451
But you're glad
I will be taken care of.

00:10:20.119 --> 00:10:21.387
And good luck on your travels.

00:10:23.622 --> 00:10:25.858
THOMAS:<i> I was interested to play
 a young guy</i>

00:10:25.891 --> 00:10:28.060
who is trying to find his way
in a world

00:10:28.127 --> 00:10:30.062
that he didn't quite know,
but do it with, you know,

00:10:30.129 --> 00:10:32.164
a chin up
and hardworking attitude.

00:10:32.198 --> 00:10:34.299
I think there are clear rules
in that society

00:10:34.333 --> 00:10:36.302
at that point in time for sure.

00:10:36.335 --> 00:10:39.138
<i> He's walking into a world
that has old ways that are set,</i>

00:10:39.171 --> 00:10:41.974
<i> and he's not particularly
 accepted off the bat.</i>

00:10:42.007 --> 00:10:45.077
We only receive the old people
in this house.

00:10:45.978 --> 00:10:47.078
Never the new.

00:10:47.646 --> 00:10:50.816
He has to work quite hard
to ingratiate himself, I guess,

00:10:50.849 --> 00:10:53.218
for lack of a better word,
into that society.

00:10:53.252 --> 00:10:56.255
<i> There's a clear difference
 between the old money
 and the new money.</i>

00:10:56.321 --> 00:10:58.156
Not that he comes from either.

00:10:58.190 --> 00:10:59.892
I think he comes
from no money, really.

00:10:59.925 --> 00:11:01.860
And that's what's
so interesting about him

00:11:01.893 --> 00:11:04.096
is that he has a wonderful arc,
really,

00:11:04.162 --> 00:11:05.998
starting from humble beginnings,

00:11:06.598 --> 00:11:09.902
<i> and through hard work,
he manages to climb that ladder.</i>

00:11:09.935 --> 00:11:12.203
New York has plenty to offer
to the new arrival.

00:11:12.671 --> 00:11:14.706
But what would I have to offer
New York?

00:11:14.739 --> 00:11:19.411
It's just a joy to come to work
and to work on these characters
and in this world.

00:11:19.778 --> 00:11:21.947
There's so much depth
to everything.

00:11:22.013 --> 00:11:24.550
MARIAN BROOK:
I wish I understood
what brings you to New York.

00:11:24.583 --> 00:11:26.285
Simple. I want to be here.

00:11:26.351 --> 00:11:28.520
-And that's enough of a reason?
-Of course it is!

00:11:28.787 --> 00:11:31.089
When I see what I really want,
I take it if I can.

00:11:32.424 --> 00:11:34.560
SIMON:
<i>One of the interesting aspects,
 I think, of households,</i>

00:11:34.593 --> 00:11:35.594
<i> were the armies of servants.</i>

00:11:35.628 --> 00:11:37.396
You had to rely
on their discretion,

00:11:37.563 --> 00:11:39.098
and I think Bannister

00:11:39.131 --> 00:11:41.033
rather prides himself
on his discretion.

00:11:41.066 --> 00:11:42.968 line:0%
♪ (ORCHESTRAL MUSIC PLAYS) ♪

00:11:44.269 --> 00:11:46.705
Welcome to East 61st Street,
Ms. Marian.

00:11:46.738 --> 00:11:49.307
My name is Bannister,
Mrs. van Rhijn's butler.

00:11:49.374 --> 00:11:53.445
Bannister, the butler, is
the backbone of the Brook house,

00:11:53.479 --> 00:11:55.247
<i> which is owned
 by Mrs. van Rhijn</i>

00:11:55.714 --> 00:11:57.649
<i> and her sister Ada.</i>

00:11:57.983 --> 00:11:59.885
Mrs. van Rhijn makes it
quite clear

00:11:59.918 --> 00:12:03.288
who is the boss of the house,
and I'm in command of the staff.

00:12:03.322 --> 00:12:05.490
I wonder if they'll try
to set him up with Ms. Marian.

00:12:05.557 --> 00:12:07.325
He wants an heiress,
the richest he can find,

00:12:07.392 --> 00:12:09.628
and Ms. Marian
hasn't a penny to her name.

00:12:09.661 --> 00:12:13.064
If you've finished
demolishing the characters
of every member of this family,

00:12:13.098 --> 00:12:14.399
John and I will serve luncheon.

00:12:15.066 --> 00:12:17.403
We have a very compact staff,
I think.

00:12:18.003 --> 00:12:20.005
Somehow, we keep the house
running very efficiently,

00:12:20.071 --> 00:12:22.908
<i> thanks to my</i> steady<i> gaze.</i>

00:12:23.175 --> 00:12:24.076
(BELL RINGS)

00:12:24.109 --> 00:12:26.244
Come on, John. Look lively.

00:12:26.278 --> 00:12:27.579
I think perhaps
he has a self-image

00:12:27.913 --> 00:12:30.816
of being the general authority
senior figure of the staff.

00:12:30.849 --> 00:12:32.985
<i> I think his natural
 Britishness makes him</i>

00:12:33.018 --> 00:12:35.120
<i> come across occasionally
 as rather cold and chilly,</i>

00:12:35.153 --> 00:12:36.988
but that helps
with his authority.

00:12:37.022 --> 00:12:38.657
I don't want trouble
in the house,

00:12:38.690 --> 00:12:40.259
and while she may not want
to cause it,

00:12:40.292 --> 00:12:41.626
she may be the cause of it.

00:12:41.660 --> 00:12:44.530
I repeat. It is not for us
to have an opinion.

00:12:44.596 --> 00:12:45.998
Certainly, not that one.

00:12:46.031 --> 00:12:48.534
What I found very extraordinary
is the people with money

00:12:48.600 --> 00:12:51.469
<i> how uncomfortable their lives
 nonetheless were</i>

00:12:52.003 --> 00:12:53.338
<i> from the way they dressed,</i>

00:12:53.371 --> 00:12:55.273
<i> from the formality
 of their behavior,</i>

00:12:55.307 --> 00:12:58.177
<i>the extraordinary social rituals
 they had to endure.</i>

00:12:58.210 --> 00:13:00.178
It was routine.
That was how it was done.

00:13:00.812 --> 00:13:03.215
(GROANS, CHUCKLES)
Does seem terrible chore.

